            }
          ]
        }
     ]
   }
 ]
}

But need also 4-aminocinnamic acid SMILES: O=C(C=Cc1ccc(N)cc1)N? Wait that's amide? Actually 4-aminocinnamic acid is O=C(O)C=Cc1ccc(N)cc1. Yes acid: O=C(O)C=Cc1ccc(N)cc1. And p-phenylenediamine: Nc1ccc(N)cc1.

Thus amide coupling uses acid and amine. So intermediate aniline is product of amide coupling: Nc1ccc(/C=C/C(=O)Nc2ccccc2N)cc1? Wait the amide attaches to the amine of p-phenylenediamine, leaving other amine free. So product SMILES: Nc1ccc(/C=C/C(=O)Nc2ccccc2N)cc1. Yes.

